WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:07.710
- Okay, thanks. My name's Christian Grose.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.210
From the University of
Southern California.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.290
I'm gonna be talking a little bit

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.920
about random lotteries and
redistricting commissions

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.850
in the United States,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:15.900
and this is really fun

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.330
because, in my early
career, I spent a lotta time

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.310
researching redistricting

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.490
with no random component, no lotteries.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.750
And then a lotta my later stuff

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.450
has been on lotteries and randomization

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.400
and experiments in legislatures.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.800
And so this is kinda bringing
some of that together.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.840
and I'm also kind of interested

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.040
in feedback on one particular
part that I'm gonna talk about

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.690
that's still in a design phase,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:40.560
and I'm also gonna talk

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.290
about some institutional-design components

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.120
of commissions across the United States

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.850
that, to me, are like
deliberative assemblies

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.610
but that actually have policy outcomes

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.683
because they make decisions over mapping.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.730
Okay, and just a little
background on what I'm gonna do.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.230
So, I'm gonna give you some context

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.850
about what's going on
in the United States,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.300
where are citizens used to draw maps

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.870
for legislative and
congressional and other maps.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.470
I'm gonna then present a research design

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.913
that I'm in the middle of and
that I would love feedback on.

00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:14.880
I have no data and no results.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.500
I have data. I have no results.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.120
It's a natural experiment,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.670
so the data exists, but I
don't have the results yet,

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.670
and I would love feedback on that.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.770
And then the second study
I'm gonna talk about

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.170
is one I've done that
is two field experiments

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.570
where people were encouraged to apply

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.387
for citizens' redistricting commissions.

00:01:33.387 --> 00:01:35.760
And so we talked about this
a little bit this morning.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.020
It gets at who are the people

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.320
that select into wanting to
serve in citizen assemblies,

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.450
wanting to serve in citizen commissions,

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.940
and then trying to encourage
the types of people

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.620
who might engage in uptake
for those commissions.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.000
And then at the end, I'm
gonna talk a little bit

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.530
about some qualitative stuff I've learned

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.430
from doing this research,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.830
from also thinking about
these questions over time,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.320
talking to people in
California and Michigan

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.820
about processes that happened there

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.310
with their citizens'
redistricting commissions.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.080
There's a little bit of
backlash to lotteries

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.880
that has come up qualitatively

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.830
that I think would be interesting
to talk about at the end.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.483
Okay, so, a little bit of the background.

00:02:14.285 --> 00:02:15.930
I mean, I think what the people

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.780
who do democratic deliberation
and citizen assemblies...

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.270
I think the redistricting
commissions are really nice models

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:22.800
because; here's a picture

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.000
of the Michigan Redistricting Commission;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.730
it's the same sorta Zooms
that you've been showing

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.350
for citizens' assemblies, right?

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.750
The pictures of the commissions
are almost identical

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.243
to what we're seeing with
citizens' assemblies,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.660
and these are done in
a handful of locations.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.900
So, redistricting commissions
redraw, typically,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.050
the US house lines,
state legislative lines,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.170
and then occasionally, at
the local level, other lines

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.720
in a small number of states
in the United States.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.360
Most states are drawn by
legislatures themselves,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.193
but in some states,

00:02:52.193 --> 00:02:57.000
citizens are drawing the lines
and serving on commissions.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:57.833
It is growing,

00:02:57.833 --> 00:03:00.030
and I'll talk about a
couple examples of that.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.290
And then the thing I think

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.180
that's most important to this conference

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.270
is that the citizens'
commissions to do redistricting

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:07.320
incorporate lotteries,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.000
and they incorporate
lotteries in different ways.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.260
Some of them incorporate lotteries

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.540
at the beginning of the process,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.550
and others incorporate
it later in the process,

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.490
after there's been a lotta
selection on other criteria,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.890
and they also serve for
a fixed period of time,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.480
but unlike citizens' assemblies,

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.220
they serve for, typically,
a decade, right?

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.750
They redraw lines, and then they're around

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:25.680
in case there's lawsuits

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.110
and they gotta come back
and draw lines again,

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.900
so it's actually a fairly...

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.030
It's got some fairly similar parallels

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.260
to a citizens' assembly,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.610
where citizens are brought in

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.320
and they've never really participated

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.470
in any, like, major
government decision-making,

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.240
like, by law they're not allowed to,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.093
but then they persist
for a while if need be.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.610
All right, so, a little bit of background

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:48.630
on where does this happen.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.460
Here's a map of the United States,

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:54.240
and the four states in the
light blue I wanna point out.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.640
California, Arizona,
Colorado, and Michigan

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.850
all have what I would call
our independent commissions.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.890
Arizona's is sorta independent.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.090
I think it's in a different category,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.240
but the three sorta true
citizens' commissions

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.640
are California, Colorado, and Michigan.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.600
California's started in 2010

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.320
for the 2010 redistricting process.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.243
The second one happened in 2020.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.830
Colorado used a citizens'
commission process in 2020

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:20.940
for the first time.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.210
They have separate commissions
for their congressional lines

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.680
than their state legislative lines.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.890
Both of those commissions have
some random-draw component

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:30.150
to the people that end up serving

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.550
on the commissions in Colorado.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.820
And then in Michigan, also used
for the first time in 2020,

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.100
and in Michigan, there's 13 commissioners.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.690
All of them are randomly assigned

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.450
with some blocking and weighting

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.030
on party and some other
demographic characteristics.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:46.950
So there's three states

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.620
that the redistricting process
is actually very reflective

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:54.180
of a deliberative citizens'
assembly-style thing.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.590
And then there's some other commissions

00:04:55.590 --> 00:05:00.150
in places like Virginia, New York.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.740
They're not really citizens' commissions.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.320
They're often appointed, or
they're some sort of hybrid,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.620
appointed by people who
are elected officials.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:08.453
I'm gonna talk mostly

00:05:08.453 --> 00:05:10.920
about design in California,
Colorado, and Michigan.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.370
All right, and so,
what're the policy choices

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.270
that they make, right?

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.970
And so they deliberate
like citizens' assemblies,

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.230
but they also make policy choices.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.720
They make pretty
significant policy choices.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.610
They're narrow policy
choices in the sense of...

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:24.600
It's a pretty niche area

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.553
to focus on mapping and redistricting,

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.420
but basically, they create
and they vote on new maps

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.280
for legislative districts
following the census.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.720
That's, like, the big policy outcome

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.840
that these citizen commissions do.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.640
They also make compliance choices, though,

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:39.900
that have pretty big impacts

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.540
on, you know, cost to the states.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.090
There's often lawsuits
around redistricting.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.240
Their decisions could affect
if the state gets sued or not

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:49.920
for the maps that they choose.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.230
They also have to comply with
the Federal Voting Rights Act.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.750
They have to comply with
state-level criteria.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.810
And so there's a lotta complex material

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:58.800
that they have to address,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:05:59.970
even though they are citizens

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.010
who're engaged in the process.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.530
And then they have lots of deliberation.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.347
They have to deliberate over
what're the communities,

00:06:06.347 --> 00:06:08.040
what're called communities of interest,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.420
which is a term around redistricting

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:10.980
that defines, like, what is an area

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.330
that people who are citizens think

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.400
is important and relevant.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.440
And so they have to go back
and forth with each other,

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.450
and they also take a
lot of public comment,

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:19.620
much more public comment

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.230
than is done when
legislatures draw the lines,

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:23.430
and that deliberation,

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.630
both amongst themselves

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:26.160
and then with the public, I think,

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.770
distinguishes the deliberative process

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:29.790
on the redistricting commissions

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:33.393
from how it's done elsewhere.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:35.333
All right, and just a little bit

00:06:35.333 --> 00:06:37.263
on the quantity of deliberation.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.640
These four bars represent
four different states.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.220
Only one of them is a
citizens' commission.

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:46.530
California's independent
citizens' commission is listed

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:47.760
at the top.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.380
The bottom axis is the number

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.860
of public hearings

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.220
that they held over time,

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.140
or excuse me, this is hours not number.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.870
That's the hours of public
hearings that they held

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.880
over the course of 2020 through 2022

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.500
about the redistricting in their state.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:13.170
You can see California
has a significant amount.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:14.040
This is pretty similar

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.890
if you look at the other
citizens' commission states too.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.570
They spend a lotta time.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:19.650
The other states I picked

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.840
just as, you know, representative examples

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.773
of a Democratic gerrymander,
Republican gerrymander,

00:07:23.773 --> 00:07:25.710
and then a Republican gerrymander

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.840
that had some voting-rights implications.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.990
New Jersey, Democratic
gerrymander, if you see,

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.450
they had very few hours of meetings.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:35.670
This is where the legislators met,

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.170
or excuse me, there's
a politician commission

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:38.490
where the legislators appoint

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:41.670
other legislators and elected officials.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:46.670
Louisiana and Wisconsin had
the fewest of these four.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:48.300
And just in general, this is the pattern

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.700
if we would look more
broadly across the states,

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.567
There is more deliberation
among the citizens' commissions,

00:07:54.567 --> 00:07:57.780
and some of that is due to
learning and lack of expertise.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.630
They spend a lotta time
hearing from experts,

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.340
hearing from people.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:04.620
They spend a lotta time
also redoing things

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.460
that maybe legislatures
and experts might've done

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.050
more quickly and more efficiently,

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.030
so there's an inefficiency
that comes out of it

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:13.713
but a deliberative bonus.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:15.960
All right, and then these institutions

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.820
are probably expanding
in the United States.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.670
Like, just this week, in Los Angeles,

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.100
the city council, who...

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.930
I know most of you're from
the East Coast, but if...

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.820
There's, like, a big racist-type scandal

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.470
that you may've heard about a year ago.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.870
After that, the city council voted,

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.490
I think, Wednesday,

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:35.550
to put on the ballot a citizens'
commission in Los Angeles

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.200
to redraw the lines in 2030.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.070
It's sorta like the state's
process but slightly different.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:45.360
Ohio, you know, this week, is trying

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.459
to gather signatures,

00:08:47.459 --> 00:08:49.320
put on the ballot a citizens' commission

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.630
that is actually pretty
similar to the Michigan process

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.633
with a little bit of California put in.

00:08:53.633 --> 00:08:57.000
I think the citizen assembly
folks can learn a lot

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.920
from the redistricting commission folks,

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.600
but I do think the
redistricting commissions

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.150
have some unique components to it

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.750
that are a little bit
different from the assemblies.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.820
All right, so the study
one that I wanna do...

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.383
This is a picture, by the way;

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.760
oh, I forget which commission this is;

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.483
of one of the redistricting
(chuckles) commissions, yeah.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:19.890
This is something I haven't designed.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.180
It's a natural experiment.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:22.920
I'd like to just present my ideas

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:26.550
and get feedback from people
on some measurement issues,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.710
and also, for those of
you who're not academics

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.630
who're interested in sort
of the institutional design

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.400
for how this works in
all the different places,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.550
we're gonna talk about some
of that in this part too.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.340
So, the question here that
I'm interested in is basically

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.680
does serving in deliberative
policy-making institutions

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.620
lead to future policy actions
at the individual level,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.090
not at the aggregate level,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.790
but, if you are a citizen
who's randomly chosen

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:50.190
to serve on a citizen's
redistricting commission,

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:53.160
do you then, in the future when
your service is complete...

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.877
Do you engage in other policy choices?

00:09:55.877 --> 00:09:57.710
Do you do things that...

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:00.630
You have learned

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:03.150
through the deliberative
and citizen process

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:04.470
how to influence policy,

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:05.840
and then you wanna try to influence policy

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:07.830
in other domains later.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:08.910
And it's a little bit different

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:10.350
than the literature on deliberation,

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:11.940
which looks at sort of what's the impact

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:12.930
on civic engagement.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:14.640
I also wanna look at that too:

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:15.870
if you're randomly chosen

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.580
to serve on a redistricting commission,

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.790
do you also then do civic
engagement down the road?

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.610
But I'm really interested
in the policy part too.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.130
Once you're picked, once
you get that random draw,

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.660
do you then decide that you wanna try

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:29.790
to have an influence
in policy in other ways

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.943
after you're no longer serving
on the citizens' commission?

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.180
And so we know little bit about this,

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.250
and we've heard about it all day,

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:39.780
you know, but there is some evidence

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:43.080
that serving in citizens
deliberative bodies

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:44.910
increases future civic engagement

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:45.743
at the individual level.

00:10:45.743 --> 00:10:47.700
You know, if you're on a jury,

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.410
you're more likely to vote in the future,

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:50.763
according to some studies.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:55.410
You know, many publics have some increase

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.400
in civic engagement.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.950
Like, Archon Fung says
that they have modest

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.743
though possibly can improve
civic engagement outcomes.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:04.860
And then you know, people
who study legislatures

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.890
have shown that people who
serve in one institution

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:08.820
early in their career

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:11.700
carry that service over, and
what they've learned there,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:12.720
into new institutions.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.640
And so I think that's possibly the case

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:15.990
with citizens' commissions.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.920
And then what I wanna do, as I mentioned,

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:23.070
is sort of go beyond this idea
there's modest improvements

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:24.030
to civic engagement

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.260
from individual citizens

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.420
participating in the
deliberative processes

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.130
but actually that there's policy gains

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:31.950
that they can make by serving.

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:34.800
And so basically, at the individual level,

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:35.790
if I apply to serve

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:37.740
on the California redistricting commission

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.110
and I'm chosen randomly,

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.690
I'm going to be more likely to get skills

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.180
during that two-year process
or that 10-year process

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:47.460
if the state is sued and
they have to come back

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:51.330
that I would not get if
I were not chosen by lot

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:54.240
to serve on the commission,

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.820
and I think some of this
is individual-level,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.090
where it's gonna change sort
of the person who's picked.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:01.680
The citizen will learn.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:04.470
But I also think some of it
will be coming to them as well,

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:07.560
like, because they've been
elevated to a different status

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.750
in terms of serving on the
citizen's commission, which has

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:14.750
a relatively high, you know, impact

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.550
and relatively high publicity

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.350
within the states that they're done.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.230
All right, so it's pretty straightforward,

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:24.210
what I'm hypothesizing here.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:25.560
You know, if you're basically drawn

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:27.300
to serve on a redistricting commission

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:30.060
where you've made policy
choices in the past,

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.680
in the future, you're
going to try to engage

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.510
in more policy action.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:34.470
Where I would like help

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.420
is how do I measure that policy action.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:38.700
And then if you're also drawn,

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:41.910
you're gonna probably engage
in future civic engagement

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:44.430
as we would expect in the
literature on deliberation.

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.620
And so here the unit of
analysis is not the commission

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.503
but each individual person
who applies to serve.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:52.770
All right, and so, the measurement

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.170
that I'm interested in here

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.450
is the future policy action.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:58.860
It's sort of tricky,

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.550
what the policy action might actually be.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.620
And then I'm also interested
in future civic engagement,

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:05.940
and I might do a survey

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:07.440
with people who've applied

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.210
and people who have served
on redistricting commissions

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:11.310
who're randomly chosen or not,

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:13.380
but I'm not sure about that.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:14.520
And basically, what I'm gonna do

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:15.480
is compare the applicants

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.070
who're randomly assigned
to serve on a commission

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:18.090
to those that were not

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:19.650
and then look at these policy outcomes.

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:20.940
And so I'm looking for ideas

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:22.230
for some of these outcome measures

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:23.880
that we could talk about.

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:25.440
All right, couple ideas I have already

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:29.040
is just, you know, engaging
in policy more broadly

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.023
in the community, writing op-eds,

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:37.410
writing about public policy
and/or civic engagement

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:38.730
once you've served.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:40.560
You know, if you're picked to do this,

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.420
you then might want to go and write

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.820
about what fair processes would be,

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.970
what would be better
deliberative processes

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:48.210
in other domains,

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:50.160
how you work with other people,

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:53.550
your ideas about redistricting
10 years later, right,

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.440
maybe even going to Ohio

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.840
to campaign on behalf of
their ballot initiative

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.400
if you've served in Michigan,

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:00.233
things like that,

00:14:00.233 --> 00:14:01.950
if you're picked to be on it

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:03.540
versus those that were not.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:04.770
It's harder to measure this.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:05.760
You know, I'm gonna try to measure this

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.680
via just straightforward Google searches

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.550
of news and op-eds and then
also, like, maybe LexisNexis,

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:14.220
and this would be the applicants
who were randomly picked

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.410
and the applicants that
were not randomly picked.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:17.490
The other one that I think'd be

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:18.450
a little bit harder to measure,

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.270
but I would like to 'cause
I think it's very likely,

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:22.260
is are they participate

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.883
in public forums and panels, right?

00:14:24.883 --> 00:14:25.740
These're people that...

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:27.300
Once they've served on these commissions,

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.970
they're going to try to
do more in the future,

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:32.453
and that's something else
that I wanna try to measure.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.990
One other one that I wanna measure

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.600
is what do they do afterwards, right?

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:41.730
So I think that serving on
these citizens' commissions

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:44.103
will increase their
public service motivation.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:46.650
I'd like to look at their career changes,

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.060
and this is a relatively small group

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:49.320
of people who end up applying

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:50.340
for redistrict commissions

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:52.260
and then either randomly
being picked or not.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:53.610
The people who are picked to serve,

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:54.720
do they go into nonprofit?

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:56.310
Do they go into advocacy?

00:14:56.310 --> 00:14:58.050
Do they leave the private sector?

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:59.880
Or do they stay in the positions

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:00.900
that they were in previously?

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:02.070
And my hypothesis would be

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.930
that, once they've served
in a citizen commission,

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:07.410
they may look towards
government, advocacy, nonprofit

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.150
more than the private sector,

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.200
having had an impact.

00:15:12.023 --> 00:15:13.775
- [Audience Member] Yeah,
it's just clarification.

00:15:13.775 --> 00:15:17.490
Are all of those commissions
that you were talking about...

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:19.560
Does this selection start in same way

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:22.272
in which the (indistinct)
commission starts?

00:15:22.272 --> 00:15:25.470
- No. I'm gonna get to that
in about two slides, yeah.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.413
And I would also like your
help there, (laughs) yeah.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:31.590
Okay, and then a couple
other things that I can't do,

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:33.510
but I wanna mention it, 'cause
people have suggest this.

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:34.920
So, behavior outcomes we often use

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:35.880
to measure civic engagement

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:40.323
is voting, you know,
serving, running for office,

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.260
donating to candidates, right?

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:44.460
All of these things are...

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:45.900
Well, voting's not a prohibition.

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:48.330
Donating to candidates
and running for office

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:49.380
is often prohibited

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.680
if you agree to serve
on these commissions,

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.750
and it's also a precondition

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:58.920
of applying and being
able to serve altogether.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:00.480
And then these are also high...

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:04.050
These're people that're sort
of high civic-engaged already

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:05.730
by choosing to apply, right?

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:07.620
And so they're people who
are turning out to vote.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:08.910
In some states, they're required

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:11.850
to have voted and had a
history of voting in the past.

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:13.143
So, these are kinda
typical behavior outcomes

00:16:13.143 --> 00:16:14.670
that we would study in political science,

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:15.660
but we can't do that here,

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:16.530
so that's why I'm looking

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.260
at some of these more
creative policy action

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:21.303
and civic engagement outcomes.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:24.540
The other thing that's a
challenge that I wanna talk about

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:25.560
is the redistricting commissions have

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:27.510
small numbers of
commissioners deliberating

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:29.760
and their processes are different
for how they're selected.

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.340
There is a randomization in the lottery

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.920
in all of these cases
that I've talked about

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:35.910
in the three US states

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:40.260
and also in some localities
that I'm gonna talk about,

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:42.720
but there's a small number of people,

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:45.510
and then also, those rules vary slightly.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:46.903
All right, so, here's an
example in California.

00:16:46.903 --> 00:16:48.390
I just wanna run through how it works.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:49.260
And so this is...

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:51.600
You know, what's the
randomization that's going on

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:53.430
in these districts?

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:55.530
And also, this is the state level,

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:59.130
and most of the local
redistricting commissions

00:16:59.130 --> 00:17:00.810
in California at the
county and the city level

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:03.210
also work very similarly to this.

00:17:03.210 --> 00:17:07.020
This is a picture of all
the people who served.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.300
One of them is my former PhD student,

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:10.260
which I'm very proud of,

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:11.460
that she was randomly drawn

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:13.980
to serve on the redistricting commission,

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:15.993
and she's a professor at Pomona College,

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:19.830
but the selection process is
actually pretty elaborate,

00:17:19.830 --> 00:17:22.800
so in 2020, there were about
20,000 people who applied

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:24.450
but didn't complete the
applications, right?

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:26.280
So they had to get reference letters.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:27.690
They had to explain why
they wanted to do it.

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.370
They didn't write the essay, right,

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:31.050
like, the usual stuff like that.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:32.250
They just filled out their name.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:33.780
And so the reality of who applied...

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:35.010
There were 2,003 people

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:36.540
who actually applied for the commission

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:37.560
in the state of California.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:38.670
It's a relatively small number

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:40.440
if you think about how large the state is,

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:44.583
but it's also a lot of people
in terms of raw numbers.

00:17:46.086 --> 00:17:48.086
All right, then what happens after that?

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:51.003
And this is in the state law.

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:53.610
The State Bureau of
Audits narrows the pool

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.380
to 60 finalists, right?

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:58.290
And so there's not a random
selection at this point

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:00.390
going from 2,003 to 60.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:01.770
The State Bureau of Audits...

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:03.840
There's a little bit of
randomization in the auditor stage

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.910
for which auditors get chosen

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:07.680
to actually review the application,

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.410
so there are auditors that
are drawn and block-randomized

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:11.820
based on their party registration

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:16.080
to review the applications
and move them down to 60,

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:20.160
but they evaluate quality:
"Who are these people?

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:21.990
Are they people that have some expertise?

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:23.640
Are they people that can work together?"

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:26.610
There's different criteria
that they have to use.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:30.030
This is the state auditor
drawing the random part later.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:32.670
And then the legislature
non-randomly, also,

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:34.620
reduces the pool of applicants to 36,

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:38.130
so this is where the party
leaders of each party

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:39.450
say, "I don't like this person,"

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:42.000
or "This person's a, you
know, secret Democrat"

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:42.990
or "a secret Republican,"

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:45.333
and they eliminate people non-randomly.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:49.980
And then after that, you get down to 36,

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:51.390
relatively small sample.

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.370
And then eight commissioners
are randomly chosen

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.330
from the 36,

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:55.950
using block randomization by party,

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.150
so you got three Rs, three Ds,

00:18:57.150 --> 00:19:00.810
and two people who're other
party or independents,

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:04.860
and they literally do pull bingo balls,

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:08.040
or I mean ping pong
balls or bingo numbers,

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:11.193
out of a hat or out of a thing.

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:15.660
So, what I'm gonna do is
use this and pool data

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:17.610
across the states with the commissions,

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:20.940
but we're talking about
some small numbers, right?

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:23.460
And then look at these
outcomes that I mention.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.890
So, the treatment's, like,
where you randomly picked.

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:28.710
And then of that 36 at the very end,

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:29.940
the control group would be the people

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:31.390
that weren't picked randomly.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:35.880
Okay, and so here's how it's
done in these three states

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.173
but four commissions.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:40.140
So, California I already talked about.

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:42.723
There're eight commissioners selected.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.860
There's 22 finalists not selected,

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:48.660
and the reason there's 22

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.820
is that six people are actually chosen

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:52.740
by the eight people
who're randomly picked.

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:54.990
And so there's 22 control group,

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:57.510
eight who were randomly
selected to choose.

00:19:57.510 --> 00:20:00.570
Michigan has a very different
process than California's.

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:04.620
Everyone who applies is
put into a random draw,

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:07.260
and the lottery happens there
without any sort of criteria

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:09.120
to try to move people out

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:10.950
for expertise, or so on.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:13.500
There's no quality control per se.

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:14.580
They just have to apply.

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:15.960
And then they're randomly drawn.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.440
There is some weighting

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:22.500
on gender, region, and other criteria.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:24.780
I don't know exactly how that
was done, 'cause it was...

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:27.300
A private auditing firm
was hired to do it in 2020.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.790
I'm trying to find out more about that.

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:32.790
And then they also
block-randomize on party.

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:34.920
And so there's 13 treatment,

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:37.380
people randomly in the
citizens' commission,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:42.360
147 finalist in Michigan
that were not selected.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:44.340
Colorado has different commissions

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:45.900
for the congressional
state legislative level,

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:47.550
but again, six people are randomly chosen.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:50.760
And then 144 finalists are not selected.

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:52.050
And in Colorado,

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:53.430
they also have some appointees

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:56.160
of politicians who serve with them,

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:57.510
but there's no random component of that,

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:59.700
so I'm only focusing on the citizens part.

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:00.533
So in total,

00:21:00.533 --> 00:21:02.970
if you pool across all
four of these commissions,

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:05.370
you got 33 people chosen by lot,

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:08.340
456 finalists not chosen by lot.

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:09.570
And then this is

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:11.880
where I would leverage the
control versus the treatment

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:14.190
for the policy outcomes or
the civic engagement outcomes

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:15.690
in the future.

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:17.250
One other, much larger table,

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:19.410
so just direct your eyes

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:21.633
to LA County, Santa Barbara, San Diego.

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:25.590
There are also very similar
ones at the local level

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:27.660
that I would like to
look at in California.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:29.253
They're expanding a lot, too,

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:31.740
and here it's very similar

00:21:31.740 --> 00:21:33.780
to what I talked about in California.

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:36.090
We're starting to get to where
we have a little bit more...

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:39.660
There's 54 people chosen by
lot across these localities.

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:42.320
LA County and San Diego County...

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:45.540
Not only am I interested in this

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:46.800
as a professor and researcher.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:48.090
I also was a consultant

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:51.270
for San Diego County's
redistricting process,

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:54.840
and so, they used a commission
for the first time in 2020,

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:58.500
where eight people were randomly chosen

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:01.050
and then 50 finalists, not selected.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.750
This is basically how these
institutions are designed,

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:05.100
for those who are interested in that,

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:08.820
from translating over
to citizens' assemblies,

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:09.870
but then also, I think,

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:11.850
it creates some real
leverage that I wanna look at

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:13.770
in terms of the political
science outcomes.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:16.680
My concern is, of course, these
are different institutions

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:18.000
with somewhat different designs,

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:19.860
even though there's randomization,

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.480
really small N, right?

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:23.100
And so I can pool this and look at it.

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:26.670
But then you know, I'm interested
in what feedback you have,

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:29.970
besides the sample's too small to bother,

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:32.550
that I could still try to leverage this.

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:34.950
Okay, and then I wanna talk a little bit

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:39.420
about a second set of studies
that I've done already

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:42.930
that might help you to figure
out who are these people,

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:44.550
like, who wants to serve

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:46.710
on a redistricting citizens' commission.

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:47.880
Who wants to serve in a...

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:49.920
Who wants to go to a
citizens' assembly? Right?

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.650
Who decides to apply
for something like this?

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.030
'Cause you can't be an elected official.

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:55.860
You can't run for office
in the future, right?

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:57.300
You have to be interested in politics

00:22:57.300 --> 00:22:58.290
and wanna make a difference,

00:22:58.290 --> 00:23:01.560
but you have never
displayed any other behavior

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:04.830
suggesting you wanna do
(chuckles) that in your life,

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:06.150
and you know, there's prohibitions

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:08.040
on donating to candidates
and things like that.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:10.320
So it's a really
interesting group of people,

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:13.320
and I'm trying to figure
out who these people are.

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:15.060
All right, and so this study is based

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:18.000
on two field experiments that
I did in the 2020 process

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:20.550
in California in Los Angeles County

00:23:20.550 --> 00:23:24.270
that basically examined
uptake in participation,

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:25.410
like, who applies,

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.660
and can we encourage more people to apply

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:29.110
and see who those people are.

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:32.910
All right, and so the argument
here is just basically

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:35.310
people who are publicly service motivated

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:37.080
or who have financial incentives

00:23:37.080 --> 00:23:38.910
may be willing to serve, right?

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:40.230
And so the experiment

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:41.880
is gonna test those two
different questions.

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:45.180
Do you wanna serve on a
redistricting citizens commission

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:46.050
because you find out

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:48.330
that you might get paid
and it's an additional...

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:50.580
'Cause there is limited
pay on all of these,

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:53.550
the financial incentive may
spur you to participate.

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:55.290
Or do you wanna do it
out of public service?

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:57.270
If you give them a sort of "this'll help."

00:23:57.270 --> 00:24:00.300
This is, like, a classic
civic engagement message

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:02.790
that you might do with
a GOTV-style experiment.

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:04.440
Will they serve?

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:08.250
All right, I won't go
into all of the results,

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:09.210
but the punchline is

00:24:09.210 --> 00:24:11.610
the public service
motivation encouraged people

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:13.590
to sign up and apply.

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.150
The financial incentive did not.

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:17.050
The financial incentive had no impact,

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:21.840
financial incentive
meaning just telling them

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:23.670
how much they will be
paid if they are chosen,

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:25.173
not any additional incentive.

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:29.490
The public service message
mixed with the financial message

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:31.830
also made no difference in terms of the...

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:35.313
It was similar to the
public service effect.

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:40.890
All right, and so, what
the two experiments were...

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:43.140
One, I partnered with a good
government organization,

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:45.300
who was concerned about the
number of people who would apply

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:47.310
for the California
redistricting commission,

00:24:47.310 --> 00:24:51.900
and we embedded this encouragement
experiment with them.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:53.910
We invited academic experts,

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:55.680
primarily social science professors

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.750
at University of California institutions

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:02.750
and a handful of other private
institutions in the state,

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.520
and it was fielded in summer of 2019.

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.590
Told them about the process,
gave 'em the application,

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:09.120
and gave 'em those different messages.

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.010
I did a parallel experiment

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:13.200
for the Los Angeles
County Commission only.

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:14.370
That was also in partnership

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:16.230
with a good government
organization based in LA,

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:17.163
a different group,

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:21.870
that was encouraging Asian
American and Latino members

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:23.250
of their good government organization

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:26.000
to apply, and the exact same
experiment was done there.

00:25:29.220 --> 00:25:30.300
Okay, and the outcome variable here

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:31.447
is really straightforward:

00:25:31.447 --> 00:25:33.147
"Did the person apply or not?"

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:37.200
As I mentioned, there's a control group

00:25:37.200 --> 00:25:40.560
of people who didn't
receive any message at all

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:43.020
from the non-partisan organizations.

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:45.390
There's one that got the public
service motivation message,

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:46.290
the financial message,

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:48.723
and the one that got the two together.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:54.307
This was the public service
message down here in the square:

00:25:54.307 --> 00:25:56.850
"Commissioners who apply and
are selected should be driven

00:25:56.850 --> 00:25:58.350
by motivation toward public service

00:25:58.350 --> 00:25:59.820
in order to make California a better place

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:01.080
and shape the state's future."

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:03.030
This text is pretty similar

00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:05.850
to the messaging that the
state was using itself

00:26:05.850 --> 00:26:07.200
to get people to serve.

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:09.960
It also included a brief
description, deadline,

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:12.513
links to the application,
and all of that stuff.

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:17.220
Same message with the
links and the details

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:20.130
and how you apply was
given to a different group

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:21.660
that was randomized,

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:25.260
and this just said what the
amount you would get paid

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:26.280
if you served.

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:28.680
It's actually not very high
for the number of hours,

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:29.550
if you remember that figure

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:30.450
I (laughs) showed at the beginning

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:32.010
with the total number of hour,

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:34.080
but it's not nothing either.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:36.090
It's actually substantial

00:26:36.090 --> 00:26:38.790
if you think about,
like, the median income

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:39.903
of California, right?

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:43.500
And so this is just saying
how much you may get paid

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:46.560
and what the people were
paid in the previous one,

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:48.030
and this is per diem, by the way.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:49.507
This isn't a salary, but this is...

00:26:49.507 --> 00:26:52.163
You know, there's a little
bit of a financial incentive.

00:26:52.163 --> 00:26:53.970
This is also true, right?

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:57.090
So this is actually,
like, factual information.

00:26:57.090 --> 00:26:59.280
And then both messages.

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:01.170
And so the results I mentioned.

00:27:01.170 --> 00:27:03.720
Financial incentive had no impact at all,

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:06.030
but the public service message, PSM,

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:08.490
people were 1.4 percentage
points more likely

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:11.530
to apply to serve on
the citizens' commission

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:14.010
than the control group.

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:14.970
And then you know,

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:17.160
the other conditions just
didn't make a difference,

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:19.260
and that was the case in
the statewide experiment

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:21.330
and also the case in the
Los Angeles experiment

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:23.675
with a different sample of people.

00:27:23.675 --> 00:27:24.840
- [Audience Member] Just
a very quick question.

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:26.400
Can you remind me how many hours,

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:28.587
like, per week or per year (indistinct)?

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:30.810
- Oh, yeah, so, it depends.

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:32.580
Like, in the year in which
they're redistricting,

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:34.920
it's probably 8-to-10-hour meetings

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:37.800
about twice a week, sometimes longer.

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:38.730
Like, at the very end,

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:39.630
when they're almost done,

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:41.430
like in November and December of 2021,

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:45.900
it was probably 10-hour
meetings almost every day:

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.540
Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, Friday.

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:50.220
And then they're done. And then once...

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:52.260
Like, in the case of California,

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:55.500
they completed the maps,
I think, on December 23rd.

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:58.830
And then there was very little...

00:27:58.830 --> 00:28:00.810
Then they went to Sacramento,
and they delivered the maps.

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:02.610
And then there's not much left to do.

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:04.140
I think they had a party.

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:06.450
And then there was basically meetings

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:08.070
every month or two, right?

00:28:08.070 --> 00:28:10.500
And that's more of, like,
what did they learn,

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:11.760
kinda coming together occasionally,

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:13.800
and now they hardly
ever meet at all, right?

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:16.740
But they do stand for a decade

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:19.110
in case there's a need to come back,

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:20.880
like, in case one of
the maps gets a lawsuit,

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:21.840
so like, in Michigan right now,

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:23.610
there is a lawsuit against the map

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:24.510
that's in court,

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:27.000
and if that succeeds, they
will have to come back again

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:30.690
and do 10-hour days three days a week

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:32.510
for probably about a month or so, yeah,

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:34.620
so it's a very high-intensity commitment

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:37.050
for a short period of time, yeah.

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:39.330
But it's a little bit like
the deliberative assemblies

00:28:39.330 --> 00:28:40.650
that go on for quite a long time,

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:42.745
where you bring people back.

00:28:42.745 --> 00:28:43.578
- [Audience Member] Did you look

00:28:43.578 --> 00:28:44.848
at heterogeneous treatment effects?

00:28:44.848 --> 00:28:45.681
'Cause you've assumed

00:28:45.681 --> 00:28:46.514
that, like, the baseline income

00:28:46.514 --> 00:28:49.170
that's under the midpoint
of your financial treatment

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:50.580
would be positively incentivized,

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:51.990
while above it would be...

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:53.037
And that would net out, right?

00:28:53.037 --> 00:28:55.170
- No, that's a great idea, and I have,

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:57.840
and because most of the
sample for the statewide study

00:28:57.840 --> 00:28:59.910
are public employees

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:02.880
'cause they're professors
at public universities,

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:05.610
the data on their income is available,

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:06.960
so I can do that, but I haven't,

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:09.813
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:15.000
You know, concluding, couple
things I wanna talk about,

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:16.560
like what have I learned from some of this

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:18.270
and also what have I
learned from observing.

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:19.140
Right, if you're interested

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:21.450
in randomization and
commissions, if you're interested

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:23.580
in citizens' assemblies and deliberation,

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:25.230
what can we learn from
Michigan, California,

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:27.300
Colorado, these localities?

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:28.353
So, few things.

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:31.470
You know, qualitatively,

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:35.160
there've actually been some
concerns raised by advocates,

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:38.520
and by advocates, I mean
people who were on the ground,

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:39.990
good government groups,

00:29:39.990 --> 00:29:44.670
or advocates in the sense of
groups who were interested

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:46.170
in, you know, increasing diversity

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:47.670
in the redistricting process,

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:50.130
who were concerned about voting rights.

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.900
In general, they've been considered

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:52.890
very positive, by the way,

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:53.723
so in general,

00:29:53.723 --> 00:29:56.880
the commission processes have
been rated highly by advocates

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:59.760
compared to when
legislatures draw the lines,

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:03.180
but within that, the
discussion around randomization

00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:06.330
has been, I think, a little misguided

00:30:06.330 --> 00:30:07.500
in the policy community

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:08.460
in some of the states,

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:11.523
and so, just for example, in California,

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:14.580
if you recall,

00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:18.900
is a really racially and
gender-and-disability-diverse group

00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:21.480
of people who were selected to serve;

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:24.180
however, in the first
draw of the eight people

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:27.510
who were randomly chosen in California,

00:30:27.510 --> 00:30:28.410
it was very diverse

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:30.990
in terms of Asian
American, Black, and white,

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:34.260
but there were no Latino
members who were chosen to serve

00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:35.093
through the random draw,

00:30:35.093 --> 00:30:36.690
and that's just small sample.

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:38.580
Part of it is the number
of people who applied

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:40.560
and sorta differential
in people who applied,

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:42.840
but part of it was just the small sample.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:44.573
You occasionally get these outcomes

00:30:44.573 --> 00:30:46.470
that're not demographically representative

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:48.720
when you're only randomly
drawing eight people, right?

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:49.890
And so there's a huge...

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:52.290
This is, like, a huge problem
in a state like California,

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:55.053
where nearly 40% of the
population is Latino.

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:58.710
And then the next six who're chosen,

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:00.120
nearly all of them were Latino,

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:02.940
which created demographic balance,

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:04.650
but because that happened,

00:31:04.650 --> 00:31:05.910
just that statistical quirk

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:08.520
of the, you know, small
sample and randomization,

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:11.310
a lot of Latino advocacy
groups have expressed concern

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:12.990
about randomization, right,

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:14.760
'cause of that one observed outcome,

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:16.593
even though of course randomization,

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:19.470
assuming it was done fairly,

00:31:19.470 --> 00:31:20.400
is fair, right?

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:22.560
And so that's something
that is a thing that just...

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:24.210
Whenever you're dealing with randomization

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:26.040
in deliberative groups

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:27.330
and it's a small sample

00:31:27.330 --> 00:31:30.030
and there may be a lack
of demographic diversity

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:31.560
that coincidentally happens,

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:33.610
it's gonna raise a lotta fairness issues.

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:37.620
In Michigan, I talked to some people there

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:40.560
who were concerned about randomization

00:31:40.560 --> 00:31:42.180
from a quality standpoint,

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:45.663
so because Michigan has no
vetting about who applies;

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:49.590
you just apply, and then they pick people;

00:31:49.590 --> 00:31:50.760
there were some concerns raised

00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:53.250
that some commissioners were
higher-quality than others,

00:31:53.250 --> 00:31:55.110
and unlike a deliberative assembly,

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:56.370
there's no real time to learn.

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:57.203
I mean, there's time to learn,

00:31:57.203 --> 00:31:59.130
but you really have to
start making policy choices

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:01.953
by serving as opposed to
kinda getting up to speed.

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:04.740
Couple things that're important

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:06.810
in all of these states is staff.

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:09.180
And so I think this is relevant
for deliberative assemblies

00:32:09.180 --> 00:32:11.430
and any other kinda citizens' assembly

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:12.810
that you wanna think about.

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:13.950
They all hire staff,

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:15.840
and it's their choice of
staff of who they hire,

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:17.880
but those staff can really be important

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:19.530
the same way that there's a bureaucracy

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:20.880
for an elected legislature

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:24.570
that, you know, the
legislatures will put in

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:25.560
that're semi-permanent

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:27.210
in the case of these commissions.

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:31.470
Having the experts that they
can hire can be important,

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:33.810
but then the ultimate
principal are these citizens

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:37.020
who're effectively making the decisions.

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:39.120
Also, in Michigan, the secretary of state,

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:41.850
I thought, wisely,

00:32:41.850 --> 00:32:43.920
and interesting from a
social science standpoint;

00:32:43.920 --> 00:32:45.270
she was a former professor;

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:51.290
she randomly sent invitations
to all, or a subset,

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:53.880
of people in Michigan to
encourage them to apply.

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:55.730
Trying to get my hands on those data.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:00.600
Those letters have also
received some negative feedback

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:01.830
because some people thought

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:05.190
that the government was
requiring them to apply.

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:06.750
Some people applied

00:33:06.750 --> 00:33:08.880
and then actually made
it to the next stage

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:10.260
and were like, "I don't
know what's going on.

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:11.093
I don't wanna do this."

00:33:11.093 --> 00:33:12.150
So there's a little bit of things.

00:33:12.150 --> 00:33:13.620
When you're incorporating lotteries

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:15.870
into public decision-making,

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:18.930
you know, whatever happens
in one or two anecdotes

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.120
will actually inform the views

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:22.200
of how people respond to them,

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:23.160
so just think about that,

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:24.000
for those of you thinking

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:28.320
about randomization and
democratic deliberation.

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:29.770
And this is what I mentioned.

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:33.570
There's some emerging
pushback on some of this,

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:36.090
but also, I think, positively,

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:37.890
there're a lotta good government groups

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.000
pushing these reforms.

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:42.630
Like, the Ohio proposal for
the commission has this.

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:45.310
They have lotteries embedded within it

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:49.170
because it does actually

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:52.320
increase fairness, right?

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:54.750
But then there are equity
concerns about who applies, right?

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:57.750
And so if you have an
unrepresentative set of applications,

00:33:57.750 --> 00:33:59.850
then the outcome may not appear equitable

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:01.053
or is not equitable.

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:04.770
Okay, that's all I've got.

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:06.540
I'm happy to take questions,

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:07.920
but it's just been really fun.

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:11.070
I appreciate Allie, thank
you for organizing this.

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:14.580
That's study two's of a paper
I've been meaning to work on,

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:16.740
and this caused me to get going on it.

00:34:16.740 --> 00:34:18.540
And then this conference
has made me think a lot

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:21.090
about ways of incorporating
redistrict commissions,

00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:25.173
democratic deliberation,
and citizen assemblies.

00:34:26.393 --> 00:34:29.393
(audience applauds)

00:34:30.990 --> 00:34:32.313
- [Mark] Hi, Mark Orton.

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:34.860
So, I guess I have two questions.

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:39.000
The first is can you
think of other examples

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:40.650
of similar sorts of bodies

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:43.053
to redistricting commissions

00:34:43.053 --> 00:34:45.090
that have similar properties

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:48.270
that're other examples in our society

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:50.220
where these things are used.

00:34:50.220 --> 00:34:52.320
- Do you mean, like,
having citizens in general?

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:54.330
Or, like, the randomization component

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.850
combined with some sort of
other selection process?

00:34:56.850 --> 00:34:59.280
- [Mark] I mean, I guess it's,
you know, similar bodies.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:02.670
It's, like, a kind of
independent citizens' body

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:04.200
doing a specific task

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:06.543
in the context of the government, I guess.

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:10.530
- No, off the top of my head, I cannot,

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:12.270
though I'm sure they exist, but I think

00:35:12.270 --> 00:35:14.610
my head is so in the redistricting
commissions right now

00:35:14.610 --> 00:35:15.780
that I'm not thinking about it,

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:17.280
so lemme get back to you on that.

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:18.840
- [Mark] Okay, and then
the second question is

00:35:18.840 --> 00:35:21.360
you mentioned, in Michigan,
there were concerns

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:23.640
about the quality of the people

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:26.280
'cause it was just pure random.

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:30.000
What was the experience of how
that commission functioned?

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:32.040
- Yeah, I mean, I actually
think it worked out pretty well.

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:34.200
I think, on balance,
the commission did well.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:35.250
I do think there were some people

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:36.903
who participated more heavily.

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:38.910
What ends up happening

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:40.590
is, if you have the true randomization

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:42.300
as your selection process,

00:35:42.300 --> 00:35:44.310
it probably privileges a few more

00:35:44.310 --> 00:35:46.950
of the people that just
were randomly chosen

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:50.571
who were the more active
participants, right?

00:35:50.571 --> 00:35:52.410
There are a couple people who
didn't attend as frequently.

00:35:52.410 --> 00:35:54.690
That happened with one
person in California as well.

00:35:54.690 --> 00:35:56.820
I also think some of the
criticism in Michigan

00:35:56.820 --> 00:36:00.090
is coming from people with interest

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:02.733
in the outcome of the maps.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:07.620
I mention that not because I
think the criticism is correct

00:36:07.620 --> 00:36:09.720
as much as it's something

00:36:09.720 --> 00:36:12.660
that was discussed and
came up during the process,

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:14.790
like, "Who are these people?

00:36:14.790 --> 00:36:17.940
Why is this person not
attending every meeting?" right,

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.850
so any way that you can kind of attack,

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:22.140
you know, if you're the legislature

00:36:22.140 --> 00:36:24.330
and you would like to keep the map

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:26.880
or you have your own interest

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:27.990
and you're not drawing the line,

00:36:27.990 --> 00:36:30.150
so some of it is that strategy.

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:32.760
But I also think some of it
is some the people applied

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:34.560
and, you know, they weren't really sure

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:36.900
what they wanted to do and
if they wanted to do it.

00:36:36.900 --> 00:36:38.550
And so there's also lotta drop-off.

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:39.750
So, people initially applied,

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:42.990
and then once they kinda found
out what it was really about,

00:36:42.990 --> 00:36:44.820
they didn't move on to the next stage,

00:36:44.820 --> 00:36:45.960
but in California

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:50.100
and other places where the
selection that's not random

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:53.220
weeds out people who maybe
are less interested early,

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:54.470
that's less of a problem.

00:37:00.521 --> 00:37:01.560
(audience member speaks indistinctly)

00:37:01.560 --> 00:37:03.647
- [Audience Member] This is
a theoretical (indistinct),

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:08.160
but was there any empirical evidence

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:11.580
that any groups were attempting

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:15.390
to co-opt the citizens' assembly

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.760
or feed them information

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:21.270
or, you know, make promises
to them or anything like that?

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:24.450
Or was that control well-controlled?

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:27.690
- So, there wasn't evidence
of the latter as far as I know

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:28.710
in any of the states,

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:31.170
but I do think that...

00:37:31.170 --> 00:37:32.220
And I think this is interesting

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:34.140
if we think about citizen
assemblies broadly.

00:37:34.140 --> 00:37:36.660
So, the redistrict commissions
are very transparent by law

00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:37.770
in all three of these states,

00:37:37.770 --> 00:37:38.970
and they have to be,

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:42.120
and as a result, you know,
people with interest in the maps

00:37:42.120 --> 00:37:44.070
know who the commissioners are.

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.170
And they have very strict
laws on communications

00:37:46.170 --> 00:37:47.460
on what they're allowed to do,

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:51.750
but the public comments
can become effectively

00:37:51.750 --> 00:37:54.810
public lobbying about
what other people want,

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:57.810
so I think that your first
part of the question...

00:37:57.810 --> 00:37:58.643
You know, there's evidence

00:37:58.643 --> 00:38:03.643
from the first California experience,

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:08.810
in 2010, that people were
putting forward ideas.

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:12.330
In 2020, the mayor of San Jose

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:15.390
called the commission
several times and talked

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:17.460
about how San Jose (laughs) is a community

00:38:17.460 --> 00:38:18.580
that must be together

00:38:19.590 --> 00:38:21.450
because, I think, he wanted
to run for Congress, right?

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:23.940
And so that's actually a pretty...

00:38:23.940 --> 00:38:26.280
And they didn't end up following
what he suggested either,

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:27.113
which is interesting,

00:38:27.113 --> 00:38:30.750
but there is a way to
influence these citizens,

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:32.700
but it all has to happen
in a public meeting.

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:36.090
And so to me, that is a little bit more...

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:38.880
Even though potentially
normatively concerning,

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:42.150
the empirical evidence would
say we all can observe that

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:42.983
as opposed to...

00:38:42.983 --> 00:38:44.730
And you know, if a
legislature's drawing the line

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:47.730
in Wisconsin, we can't
observe what is happening

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:49.830
and who's lobbying whom about that,

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:50.663
so I think yeah.

00:38:50.663 --> 00:38:52.350
But I think there's some
empirical evidence of that.

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:55.590
On the other hand, the negative feedback

00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:57.690
in multiple states, all
three states actually;

00:38:57.690 --> 00:38:58.680
I haven't talked about Colorado;

00:38:58.680 --> 00:39:03.240
from some of the parties and
some specific elected officials

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:05.760
would suggest that there may be

00:39:05.760 --> 00:39:07.080
less empirical evidence of that,

00:39:07.080 --> 00:39:08.880
because the negative
(chuckles) feedback came

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:10.430
after the maps were done, yeah.

00:39:14.520 --> 00:39:15.360
- [Philip] Hi, Philip Lindsay

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:18.453
from the Democracy Innovation
Hub at Bard College.

00:39:19.650 --> 00:39:20.483
Two questions.

00:39:20.483 --> 00:39:22.380
The first one is, so many times, I think,

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:24.900
because we don't really train facilitators

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:25.950
in our society at large;

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:28.440
we don't think about the facilitators;

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:29.520
they're not included.

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:33.960
I'm curious if there were
facilitators in these processes

00:39:33.960 --> 00:39:35.100
and how that played

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:37.920
into, you know, how much
time people were speaking.

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:41.130
And then the second
question is I always imagine

00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:43.110
that, if you get randomly
selected citizens,

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:45.000
you bring 'em together and
say, "Hey, you should...

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:46.410
We're gonna redraw the maps.

00:39:46.410 --> 00:39:47.820
There's all this gerrymandering,"

00:39:47.820 --> 00:39:48.653
that, at some point,

00:39:48.653 --> 00:39:50.940
some citizens would
propose some other form

00:39:50.940 --> 00:39:52.350
of proportional representation

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:53.183
for their state,

00:39:53.183 --> 00:39:54.840
and I'm wondering if that
conversation ever came up.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:55.860
Or is there just...

00:39:55.860 --> 00:39:58.890
Is it just so unconstitutional
at the state level?

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:00.180
Or there's so many hurdles

00:40:00.180 --> 00:40:03.450
that, like, the conversation
would never go near that?

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:06.093
- Yeah, that's a really good point.

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:11.850
I mean, on the first one,
you know, very slightly,

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:14.550
but on balance, there's not
what I would call facilitators.

00:40:14.550 --> 00:40:16.410
Like, so they're on there. They serve.

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:18.120
They do hire an executive director.

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:20.730
They hire legal staff, right?

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:23.520
There are staff who help
guide them in specific things,

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:26.040
but typically, they're
leading the meetings.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:26.940
And so what happened...

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:27.810
What California did

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:30.540
was they rotated the
chair of their committee.

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:32.040
They just chose to do that early on.

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:34.290
And then that person was
effectively the facilitator

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:36.210
for the other 13 commissioners

00:40:36.210 --> 00:40:37.443
during the meetings.

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:45.450
Because all of these three states

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:47.370
and then if it passes in
Ohio and the localities,

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:49.830
there's some pretty
strict rules in the law

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:52.290
about what they can talk about,

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:53.520
when it has to get done,

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:55.500
deadlines for completion.

00:40:55.500 --> 00:40:57.060
I wouldn't call that facilitation

00:40:57.060 --> 00:40:59.640
as much as they had to
have public comment.

00:40:59.640 --> 00:41:02.100
They had to be in
deliberation with each other,

00:41:02.100 --> 00:41:04.290
and then they had to be in
deliberation with the public,

00:41:04.290 --> 00:41:05.700
or at least, they had to hear the public,

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:07.200
they didn't necessarily have to respond,

00:41:07.200 --> 00:41:08.730
and then that would
repeat again and again.

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:11.670
And so because of that, the
deliberation almost happened

00:41:11.670 --> 00:41:14.190
because they were required by law,

00:41:14.190 --> 00:41:15.360
even without a facilitator,

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:17.820
but certainly, the chair
ended up facilitating a lot,

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:20.160
and you could see the
commissioner, who was the chair,

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:24.150
would often step back
and not speak as much

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:26.820
while the other 13 were talking.

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:28.650
There are also some people
who are more like...

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:31.410
They're just the leaders that
emerged through the process

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:33.960
and were a little more
dominant in the process

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:36.880
towards guiding things and undoing things

00:41:38.280 --> 00:41:40.620
in all of the different states:

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:42.540
Colorado, Michigan, and California.

00:41:42.540 --> 00:41:43.440
But on to your second question

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:44.700
is, like, "Does this
make them wanna just do

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:46.140
electoral system change?"

00:41:46.140 --> 00:41:47.070
That's a really good idea,

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:47.903
and that's kinda what I want to get

00:41:47.903 --> 00:41:49.020
out of the policy action.

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:49.853
So, they can't do that.

00:41:49.853 --> 00:41:50.857
They can't, in the commission, say,

00:41:50.857 --> 00:41:52.890
"We wanna get rid of
single-member districts.

00:41:52.890 --> 00:41:54.057
This is frustrating."

00:41:55.260 --> 00:41:57.660
They're bound by the law, but some of...

00:41:57.660 --> 00:41:58.830
Anecdotally, I do know some

00:41:58.830 --> 00:42:00.450
of the commissioners who served in 2020

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:02.310
have already attended conferences

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:05.340
about election-system change,
proportional representation,

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:06.903
multi-member districts.

00:42:07.800 --> 00:42:10.470
You know, this sort of did,

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:12.450
kinda consistent with my hypothesis...

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:14.940
I think I'm gonna measure
that outcome specifically.

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:16.230
Some of them are now working

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:18.933
on these sort of election systems,

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:23.070
broad policy changes
that they may like to see

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:25.370
or at least wanna be
part of the conversation.

00:42:31.230 --> 00:42:32.160
- [Kevin] Thanks so much, yeah.

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:35.624
So, Kevin Elliott from here at Yale.

00:42:35.624 --> 00:42:38.520
Yeah, so, just on this last point.

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:41.790
So, when you were trying to
differentiate civic engagement

00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:42.840
from policy actions,

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:44.070
I was a little puzzled at first,

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:46.500
and then when you began talking about it,

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:49.500
it became clearer 'cause I
thought about it in terms of...

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:51.120
The policy actions that
you're talking about

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:53.730
are things that're specifically
related to redistricting

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:54.630
it seems like.

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:55.463
In other words,

00:42:55.463 --> 00:42:59.493
they've acquired policy expertise,
issue expertise, on that.

00:43:00.960 --> 00:43:05.070
It seems like all of
the suggested measures

00:43:05.070 --> 00:43:07.140
had to do specifically
with their deployment

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:10.350
of this specific-issue
expertise that they had,

00:43:10.350 --> 00:43:11.730
and when you think about that

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:14.760
as opposed to more general
civic engagement...

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:17.520
That makes sense to me
as a distinction, right?

00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:19.140
So it'd be civic engagement

00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:21.540
on the thing that they are
now relative experts on

00:43:21.540 --> 00:43:24.840
as opposed to civic
engagement more broadly.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:26.100
- Yeah, that's a good point.

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:28.710
I mean, I was thinking
of civic engagement more

00:43:28.710 --> 00:43:30.300
in kinda the traditional literature

00:43:30.300 --> 00:43:31.830
of, like, how we've measured it

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:33.600
and then the policy parts separately.

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:37.170
I do think that the policy
actions that you would expect

00:43:37.170 --> 00:43:40.260
coming out of service
and being randomly drawn

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:42.420
would be on the...

00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:46.050
I'd say, like, the broader
election-system topics.

00:43:46.050 --> 00:43:49.590
It may not be about redistricting
precisely but could be;

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:51.180
however, again, I have anecdotal evidence,

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:53.640
and I've not collected the data yet,

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:54.473
that some of them

00:43:54.473 --> 00:43:58.800
have done other public
policy-style things post-service.

00:43:58.800 --> 00:43:59.700
Like, some of the people served

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:02.730
on the 2020, excuse me, the
2010, California commission,

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:05.280
who are now done, who were randomly drawn,

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:06.810
they're on panels

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:10.110
about what can California
teach Arizona, right?

00:44:10.110 --> 00:44:11.400
And it's not about redistricting.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:14.430
It's about sort of just
having policy discussions

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:16.620
and things of that nature.

00:44:16.620 --> 00:44:17.460
Some of this is also...

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:19.260
It's, like, you have to
figure out what's the supply

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:20.310
versus the demand, right?

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:23.100
Like, how much of this
is the individual level:

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:25.200
person serves, and now
they wanna go and engage

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:27.630
in policy in other domains?

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:29.730
Or is this people are now seeking them out

00:44:29.730 --> 00:44:34.320
because, you know, they
watched them, for a year, do

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:36.120
deliberative meetings with other citizens?

00:44:36.120 --> 00:44:37.830
And that's harder for me to tease out,

00:44:37.830 --> 00:44:40.230
but I do think this translates
beyond just redistricting.

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:43.110
I think it translates
to policy action broadly

00:44:43.110 --> 00:44:46.440
with the limitations of
you can't run for office,

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:47.310
I think, in all the states,

00:44:47.310 --> 00:44:49.290
for almost a decade or more.

00:44:49.290 --> 00:44:50.340
You're limited to do,

00:44:50.340 --> 00:44:53.103
like, straightforward
political activities.

00:44:53.103 --> 00:44:54.762
- [Kevin] But it's
leveraging your experience.

00:44:54.762 --> 00:44:55.604
I think that that's-

00:44:55.604 --> 00:44:56.934
- Yeah, oh yeah.
- (indistinct) common.

00:44:56.934 --> 00:44:58.365
That's still a common thread.

00:44:58.365 --> 00:44:59.580
- That's a good point, yeah.

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:01.320
So, they actually have
much more experience

00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:03.420
that they've gained via skills
that they wouldn't have had

00:45:03.420 --> 00:45:05.889
before they served, yeah, yeah.

00:45:05.889 --> 00:45:06.921
- [Moderator] Okay,
great. Thank (indistinct).

00:45:06.921 --> 00:45:09.921
(audience applauds)

